{%- comment -%}
  Hartwick Atelier — the three typographic voices.
  Source: BRANDING/Typography/"HA Typography Rules _ READ ME.pdf"

    IDENTIFY  Porter Medium              house name, headings, navigation   (uppercase A-Z only)
    NARRATE   Junicode Medium / Italic   editorial copy, headlines, quotations
    RECORD    Noto Sans Mono Condensed   specifications, provenance, dates, credits

  Porter is supplied as desktop .otf only and its web licence is unresolved (risk R1),
  so --ha-font-identify currently carries Junicode.

  There is deliberately NO @font-face for Porter here. Referencing a font file that
  is not in assets/ makes Theme Check report MissingAsset every time this file is
  opened, and a standing warning that everyone learns to ignore is worse than no
  warning at all. The checkbox below still works: it puts "Porter" at the front of
  the identify stack, which harmlessly falls through to Junicode while the family
  is undefined.

  WHEN THE LICENCE LANDS — three steps:
    1. Upload the webfont to assets as  ha-porter-medium.woff2
    2. Paste this block back at the top of the style tag:

         @font-face {
           font-family: "Porter";
           src: url({{ 'ha-porter-medium.woff2' | asset_url }}) format("woff2");
           font-weight: 500;
           font-style: normal;
           font-display: swap;
         }

    3. Tick "Porter webfont licence confirmed" in Theme settings.

  Note the brand rule: Porter is UPPERCASE A-Z only. Numbers, punctuation and
  symbols are not permitted in it — those stay in Junicode or Noto.

  Font files are Latin-subset variable WOFF2 built from the Brand Assets originals:
    Junicode  1.1 MB -> 75 KB   (wght 300-700 retained, wdth/ENLA axes pinned)
    Noto Mono 400 KB -> 12 KB
{%- endcomment -%}

{% style %}
  {%- comment -%}
    PORTER — the identify voice, now served as a webfont at Ivan's instruction.

    ⚠️ LICENCE (risk R1, docs/audit-and-plan.md). The supplied files are DESKTOP
    .otf only and the fonts declare "Envato - GraphicRiver, commercial license"
    (designer Frank Hemmekam). No webfont licence document exists in the Brand
    Assets folder. Desktop licences normally do NOT permit web embedding, and a
    webfont is publicly downloadable by design. This is served because Ivan
    instructed it after being told; the licence still needs resolving with the
    foundry before launch. Turning `ha_porter_licensed` off removes it in one
    click and the stack falls back to Junicode with no other change.

    The files are subset to UPPERCASE A-Z AND SPACE ONLY — the brand rule
    (Typography READ ME) is that Porter carries no numbers, punctuation or
    symbols. Subsetting enforces that mechanically: any other character has no
    glyph in Porter and falls through to Junicode automatically, so the rule
    cannot be broken by an author who has not read it. 22 KB of OTF becomes
    1.7 KB of WOFF2 as a result.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if settings.ha_porter_licensed -%}
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Porter";
    src: url({{ 'ha-porter-medium.woff2' | asset_url }}) format("woff2");
    font-weight: 500;
    font-style: normal;
    font-display: swap;
  }
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Porter";
    src: url({{ 'ha-porter-light.woff2' | asset_url }}) format("woff2");
    font-weight: 300;
    font-style: normal;
    font-display: swap;
  }
  {%- endif -%}

  @font-face {
    font-family: "Junicode";
    src: url({{ 'ha-junicode.woff2' | asset_url }}) format("woff2");
    font-weight: 300 700;
    font-style: normal;
    font-display: swap;
  }
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Junicode";
    src: url({{ 'ha-junicode-italic.woff2' | asset_url }}) format("woff2");
    font-weight: 300 700;
    font-style: italic;
    font-display: swap;
  }
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Noto Sans Mono Condensed";
    src: url({{ 'ha-noto-mono-cond.woff2' | asset_url }}) format("woff2");
    font-weight: 400;
    font-style: normal;
    font-display: swap;
  }
  {%- comment -%}
    RECORD MEDIUM. Noto Sans Mono Condensed is not a variable font here — it is
    a static instance, so weight 500 has to be a second file or the browser
    synthesises it (that synthesis is what made the footer policy links look
    like a mix of bold and regular; see .ha-record below).

    Added 10 September 2026 for the landing page, where Aloha specified
    "Noto Sans Mono Condensed in Medium" for the contact line. Subset from
    BRANDING/Typography/FONT/NOTO SANS MONO/NotoSansMono_Condensed-Medium.ttf
    to the same 443 codepoints as the Regular above, 18 KB. Noto is SIL OFL,
    so unlike Porter there is no licence question here.

    Nothing else on the site uses it yet — .ha-record stays on 400.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Noto Sans Mono Condensed";
    src: url({{ 'ha-noto-mono-cond-medium.woff2' | asset_url }}) format("woff2");
    font-weight: 500;
    font-style: normal;
    font-display: swap;
  }
  {%- comment -%}
    THE JOURNAL (Aloha's artboard, 23 September 2026) sets its headline
    tickers in Junicode Cond, its section nav in Junicode SemiCond SemiBold,
    and some kickers in Noto Sans Mono (normal width, Medium / SemiBold) and
    Noto Sans Mono Condensed Bold. Built by scripts/make-journal-fonts.py to
    the same characters as the files above. A browser downloads a face only
    when a page uses it, so these cost the other pages nothing.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Junicode Cond";
    src: url({{ 'ha-junicode-cond.woff2' | asset_url }}) format("woff2");
    font-weight: 400;
    font-style: normal;
    font-display: swap;
  }
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Junicode SemiCond";
    src: url({{ 'ha-junicode-semicond-semibold.woff2' | asset_url }}) format("woff2");
    font-weight: 600;
    font-style: normal;
    font-display: swap;
  }
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Noto Sans Mono Condensed";
    src: url({{ 'ha-noto-mono-cond-bold.woff2' | asset_url }}) format("woff2");
    font-weight: 700;
    font-style: normal;
    font-display: swap;
  }
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Noto Sans Mono";
    src: url({{ 'ha-noto-mono-medium.woff2' | asset_url }}) format("woff2");
    font-weight: 500;
    font-style: normal;
    font-display: swap;
  }
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Noto Sans Mono";
    src: url({{ 'ha-noto-mono-semibold.woff2' | asset_url }}) format("woff2");
    font-weight: 600;
    font-style: normal;
    font-display: swap;
  }
  :root {
    --ha-font-identify: {% if settings.ha_porter_licensed %}"Porter", {% endif %}"Junicode", Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
    --ha-font-narrate:  "Junicode", Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
    --ha-font-record:   "Noto Sans Mono Condensed", ui-monospace, "SF Mono", Menlo, monospace;

    /* ==================================================================
       WHICH VOICE CARRIES WHICH ROLE  —  Aloha, 22 September 2026

       Her "web text sizes" note restates the hierarchy with one row per text
       category, and two of those rows move a voice:

         Porter   — major titles and product names
         Junicode — editorial headings, Expressions, numbers,
                    selected introductions
         Noto Sans Mono Condensed — BODY COPY, NAVIGATION, functional
                    information, captions and data

       Body copy and the navigation/footer chrome were both in other voices
       until now: body copy in Junicode (the brand book's NARRATE role) and the
       header and footer in Porter (Aloha's own 15 September note, "footer fonts
       in Porter - Light"). Her table is the later instruction and is taken as
       the direction, so both default to the record voice here.

       Each is a single setting rather than a rewrite, because each reverses an
       earlier decision of hers and a reversal should cost one click, not a
       developer. Theme settings > Hartwick — Material Archive > Typography
       voices. Flagged to Aloha.
       ================================================================== */
    {%- liquid
      assign body_voice = settings.ha_body_voice | default: 'record'
      assign chrome_voice = settings.ha_chrome_voice | default: 'record'
    -%}
    {%- if body_voice == 'narrate' -%}
    --ha-voice-body: var(--ha-font-narrate);
    --ha-voice-body-weight: 300;
    {%- else -%}
    --ha-voice-body: var(--ha-font-record);
    /* Noto Sans Mono Condensed ships here as two static instances, 400 and 500.
       Asking a 400-only face for 300 is harmless (no lighter face is
       synthesised, the browser serves the 400) but asking for it explicitly
       states the intent and keeps the weight off the synthesis path. */
    --ha-voice-body-weight: 400;
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if chrome_voice == 'identify' -%}
    --ha-voice-chrome: var(--ha-font-identify);
    --ha-voice-chrome-weight: 300;
    {%- else -%}
    --ha-voice-chrome: var(--ha-font-record);
    --ha-voice-chrome-weight: 400;
    {%- endif -%}

    /* override Luxe's font_picker-generated families.

       --font-body-family is the family every unmarked word on the site
       inherits — Luxe's cart, account pages, forms, facets, cards and the
       cookie banner included. Pointing it at the body voice is what makes
       "Noto Sans Mono Condensed = body copy […] functional information" true
       across the whole theme rather than only in the Hartwick sections. */
    --font-heading-family: var(--ha-font-identify);
    --font-body-family:    var(--ha-voice-body);
    --font-accent-family:  var(--ha-font-record);

    /* Luxe derives these from the old Swiss 721 font picker (weight 400).
       The brand book set Junicode body copy and editorial display at Medium;
       Aloha overruled that on 18 September 2026: "All Junicode fonts are always
       Light, and when it's in Italics, it's also Light Italic." Junicode is
       therefore 300 wherever it appears, and so is its bold — <strong> in
       Junicode no longer changes weight (the italic face is the emphasis
       available). The variable file still carries 300-700 because the landing
       page's headline is set in Junicode Bold by its own design and is not
       part of this rule.

       The body weight now follows the body voice: 300 while Junicode carries
       body copy, 400 once Noto does. */
    --font-body-weight: var(--ha-voice-body-weight);
    --font-body-weight-bold: var(--ha-voice-body-weight);
    --font-body-style: normal;
    /* Price is a record row in Aloha's table (12px, Noto), so it takes the
       record weight. It followed Junicode's 300 while prices were set in
       Junicode. */
    --price-font-weight: 400;
  }

  /* No synthetic bold in any voice. The browser default for <strong> and <b>
     is "bolder", which lifts Junicode Light (300) to 400 — that is what put the
     shipping note's opening sentence a weight above its paragraph — and which
     makes the browser fake a bold for Noto, whose 700 is not loaded. Emphasis
     keeps its parent's weight everywhere; italic remains the emphasis available
     in Junicode. Shopify's cookie-consent banner sets its own heading bold,
     hence the id-qualified override. */
  strong, b { font-weight: inherit; }
  #shopify-pc__banner h2 { font-weight: inherit; }

  /* IDENTIFY — brand book: uppercase only, tracking +20 to +80, line height 90-110%

     `!important` on the family is deliberate and is the only one of the three
     voices that needs it. Luxe's base.css carries

         button, .button, .link--underlined, .product-form__input,
         div[role="button"], .shopify-challenge__button, .account-sub-navigation
         { font-family: var(--font-accent-family) !important; }

     which put every primary control — Add to Bag, Join The Register, the
     appointment submit — into the RECORD voice no matter what class we gave it.
     A voice class that any theme rule can silently overrule is not a voice
     class. `.ha-identify` is our own opt-in marker, applied only where Porter is
     wanted, so making it authoritative is safe and fixes every current and
     future case at once rather than one button at a time. */
  .ha-identify {
    font-family: var(--ha-font-identify) !important;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  /* NARRATE — brand book: body line height 140-155%, tracking 0 to +10.
     Always Light (see the weight note above): stated here so that a heading
     given this voice takes 300 rather than Luxe's font-picker heading weight. */
  .ha-narrate {
    font-family: var(--ha-font-narrate);
    font-weight: 300;
    line-height: 1.45;
    letter-spacing: 0.005em;
  }

  /* RECORD — brand book: uppercase labels, tabular numerals, left aligned.

     `font-weight: 400` is explicit because Noto Sans Mono Condensed ships in
     ONE weight here and Luxe's `--font-body-weight` asks for 500. Asking a
     400-only face for 500 makes the browser synthesise bold, which it does
     unevenly from run to run — that is why the footer policy links looked like
     a mix of bold and regular. Requesting the weight the file actually has
     removes the synthesis entirely. */
  .ha-record {
    font-family: var(--ha-font-record);
    font-weight: 400;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  }

  /* NAVIGATION. The typography rules made this an IDENTIFY surface ("house
     identification, principal headings, navigation, collection titles") and it
     ran in Porter until now; Aloha's 22 September table moves the navigation row
     to Noto Sans Mono Condensed, so it follows --ha-voice-chrome and defaults
     there. Luxe's base.css assigns --font-accent-family to .header-bar-inner
     with !important; these selectors are more specific, so they win either way.
     Buttons and other interface detail were already on RECORD. */
  .hdr-nav-primary-level-ul a,
  .nav-ul--primary a,
  .nav-ul--secondary a,
  /* the utility side of the bar is navigation as well — Bag, Account, Search */
  .header-bar .cart-icon--bubble,
  .header-bar a[href*="/cart"],
  .header-bar a[href*="/account"],
  /* the shopping-tools labels — Search, Account, Bag. Search renders as
     `.hdr-st-item-label` inside a <button>, so it was still being caught by
     Luxe's `button { … !important }` rule and sitting in the RECORD voice while
     the links either side of it were in Porter. */
  .header-bar--shopping-tools .hdr-st-item,
  .header-bar--shopping-tools .hdr-st-item-label,
  .hdr-search-btn .hdr-st-item-label,
  .cart-count-bubble {
    font-family: var(--ha-voice-chrome) !important;
    /* UPPERCASE IS NOT DECORATION HERE — it is required while Porter carries
       this surface.

       Porter is subset to A-Z and space (the brand rule: no numbers,
       punctuation or symbols). A label typed in sentence case therefore renders
       its capital in Porter and every lowercase letter in Junicode, because the
       glyphs simply are not in the file. "Account" — which comes from a
       translation string, not from the menu — was rendering as "A" in Porter
       and "ccount" in Junicode, which is what made it look like a different
       font and size from the menu items above it.

       Uppercasing every Porter surface guarantees a single face. Any label an
       editor types in mixed case is covered automatically. */
    text-transform: uppercase;
    /* Porter Light, to match the footer (Aloha, 15 September 2026: "not too
       big… a clean look"; the Medium read as bold beside it). The 300 face is
       declared above; where Porter is not licensed Junicode carries 300 too.
       Noto has no 300, so the token resolves to 400 on that voice. Luxe sets
       the bar's weight with !important, hence the same here. */
    font-weight: var(--ha-voice-chrome-weight) !important;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    /* Aloha's table: navigation is 11px, line height 1.2. Luxe's header section
       sets its own sizes from section settings, which are set to match; this is
       the backstop for any nav surface the section does not name. */
    font-size: var(--ha-t-nav);
    line-height: var(--ha-t-lh-nav);
  }

  /* Wordmark. Colour comes from `color` because the SVG paints with currentColor:
     Inkstone on light grounds, Chalk Blue on dark. Logo rules give a 220px digital
     minimum width — below that the framed House Symbol must be used instead. */
  .ha-wordmark {
    display: block;
    width: 220px;
    max-width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 1225.9 / 301.7;
    color: var(--ha-inkstone);
  }
  .ha-wordmark--invert { color: var(--ha-chalk-blue); }
{% endstyle %}
